@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Markers
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Markers

@*
	Everything behind a pin that the map itself cannot show: the icon's name, the note, the
	photograph, and the delete (§16.4, §16.5).

	Two callers, deliberately one component - the live ride page's sidebar list and its
	tap-a-pin popup show the same marker and must show it the same way. The alternative was
	two copies of the note/photo/delete block, which drift.

	No <style> block here on purpose. The only page that mounts this owns the `.marker-body`
	rules, and a component that can render several times over would otherwise emit the same
	<style> element once per instance.
*@

<div class="marker-body">
	<p class="kind">@MarkerIconGlyphs.Label(Marker.Icon)</p>

	@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Marker.Note))
	{
		@*
			Plain text, never HTML or Markdown (§16.2) - LinkedText is the one component allowed to
			disagree, and it does so by emitting an anchor per URL run rather than by injecting
			markup, so the rule above is unchanged.

			§17.2 used to exclude marker notes from linkifying and gave no reason for it. The two
			exclusions that do have reasons are about truncated blurbs - a cut can land mid-URL, and
			a list row is its own tap target. Neither is true here: this is full-length prose in a
			panel that already carries its own buttons. A note saying "details at <url>" is the
			normal case for a meeting point.
		*@
		<p class="note"><LinkedText Text="@Marker.Note" /></p>
	}
	else
	{
		<p class="hint">No note.</p>
	}

	@if (Marker.PhotoId is { } photoId)
	{
		@* An <img src> cannot carry a bearer token, so the bytes are fetched and inlined (§16.4). *@
		@* Name, not Title: an untitled marker (§16.2) would otherwise give the photo an empty
		   alt, which reads to a screen reader as an image nobody bothered to describe. *@
		<AuthedImage Url="@($"/api/v1/photos/{photoId}")"
					 Alt="@MarkerIconGlyphs.Name(Marker.Title, Marker.Icon)" Class="marker-photo" />
	}

	@*
		Reporting a pin, not only a post (§10.2, §16.5). A marker's title, note and photograph are
		user-generated content that every member of the adventure can see, so App Store guideline
		1.2 wants the same flag control the thread carries. Never on the reader's own marker -
		reporting yourself is not a thing, and they have the delete instead.
	*@
	@*
		The flag alone, no caption. It sits beside a red "Delete marker", and two captioned buttons
		on one row read as two equal choices - which they are not: deleting is the organiser's
		routine housekeeping, reporting is rare and goes to the operator. The glyph is the same one
		the thread uses on a post, so it means the same thing in both places.
	*@
	<div class="marker-actions">
		@if (CanReport)
		{
			<button type="button" class="icon-button report" disabled="@Reporting"
					title="Report marker" @onclick="() => OnReport.InvokeAsync(Marker)">
				<span aria-hidden="true">🚩</span>
				<span class="visually-hidden">Report marker</span>
			</button>
		}

		@if (CanDelete)
		{
			<button type="button" class="danger" disabled="@Deleting" @onclick="() => OnDelete.InvokeAsync(Marker)">
				@(Deleting ? "Deleting…" : "Delete marker")
			</button>
		}
	</div>
</div>

@code {
	/// <summary>The marker being shown.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired] public MarkerDto Marker { get; set; } = default!;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether to offer the delete. The caller decides - this component has no view of who is
	/// signed in, and the server enforces the rule regardless (§16.5).
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public bool CanDelete { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Whether this marker's delete is already in flight.</summary>
	[Parameter] public bool Deleting { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Raised when the delete is pressed. Confirmation is the caller's business.</summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MarkerDto> OnDelete { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether to offer the report. The caller decides, for the same reason <see cref="CanDelete"/>
	/// is the caller's: this component has no view of who is signed in, and the server checks the
	/// membership again regardless (§16.5).
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public bool CanReport { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Whether this marker's report is already in flight.</summary>
	[Parameter] public bool Reporting { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Raised when the report is pressed. Confirmation is the caller's business.</summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MarkerDto> OnReport { get; set; }
}
